title: One undo
intro: Edit → Undo and Ctrl+Z go to whatever you worked on last: the scene, a table, a chart, a simulation, or the sprite you're painting. Each keeps its own steps.
project: Empty | One Undo

chapter: A table and the scene | Each keeps its own steps
deselect
create: Sprite
rename: Chest
set: Sprite.Order = 3
expectundo: Change Order
menu: Data > New Table...
prompt: Loot
cell: Row 1 > Value = 5
click: tables:cell:Row 1 > Name
expectundo: Set Row 1's Value in the Loot table
explain: tables:grid | The table was changed last | Ctrl+Z goes to what you worked on last: here, the Loot table. The status bar says what was undone, and where.
key: Ctrl+Z
expectcell: Row 1 > Value =
expectvalue: Chest.Sprite.Order = 3
say: The chest keeps its order. The scene has steps of its own.
select: Chest
expectundo: Change Order
explain: panel:Hierarchy | Back to the scene | Click into the Hierarchy, the Scene view or the Inspector, and Ctrl+Z goes to the scene again.
key: Ctrl+Z
expectvalue: Chest.Sprite.Order = 0
expectcell: Row 1 > Value =

chapter: A sprite | In the Sprite Painter
menu: Art > New Sprite...
painter
paint: 4,4 > 11,4
expectundo: Paint in the Sprite Painter's Sprites/sprite.png
say: The Sprite Painter is a window of its own. Ctrl+Z there takes back a stroke at a time, and so does Edit, Undo.
key: Ctrl+Z
expectundo: nothing
key: Ctrl+Y
expectundo: Paint in the Sprite Painter's Sprites/sprite.png
say: Agents use the same undo: one tool, Edit Undo, going wherever you'd go.
